            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance Mars rover has **completed a “marathon” on Mars**, reaching **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on the Red Planet. It hit the milestone on its **1,890th Martian day, or sol**, after about **five years and four months** of driving—much faster than Opportunity, which took **11 years and two months** to reach the same distance【5:3†source】.\n\nNASA also released a new orbital image from the **Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter’s HiRISE camera**, taken **June 13, 2026**, showing Perseverance as a tiny green speck with its tracks visible across the Martian surface. At the time, the rover was operating **west of Jezero Crater** in an area the science team calls **“Arbot”**【5:3†source】.\n\nPerseverance remains an **active mission** focused on searching for signs of ancient microbial life and collecting Martian rock/regolith samples for possible future return to Earth【5:4†source】. A major scientific highlight noted by NASA is the rover’s investigation of the “Cheyava Falls” rock and its “Sapphire Canyon” sample, which NASA describes as containing **potential biosignatures**—clues that could suggest past life, though more study is needed before drawing conclusions【5:4†source】.",
